This PR has been triggered by #298. I used [BenchmarkDotNet](https://benchmarkdotnet.org/) to compare DynamicExpresso and Ncalc for the kind of expression reported in #298:

```c#
using BenchmarkDotNet.Attributes;
using DynamicExpresso;
using NCalc;

namespace Benchmark;

public static class Program
{
	public static void Main()
	{
		BenchmarkDotNet.Running.BenchmarkRunner.Run<DynamicExpressoVsNCalcBenchmark>();
	}
}

public class DynamicExpressoVsNCalcBenchmark
{
	private string _expressionText;
	private Interpreter _dyn;

	[Params(5, 20)]
	public int N;

	[GlobalSetup]
	public void Setup()
	{
		_dyn = new Interpreter();
		_dyn.SetFunction("Al", (Func<string, bool>)(id => false));
		_expressionText = string.Join(" || ", Enumerable.Range(1, N).Select(i => $"Al(\"id{i}\")"));
	}

	[Benchmark]
	public System.Linq.Expressions.Expression<Func<bool>> DynamicExpressoParsingOnly()
	{
		return _dyn.ParseAsExpression<Func<bool>>(_expressionText);
	}

	[Benchmark]
	public bool DynamicExpressoEval()
	{
		return _dyn.Eval<bool>(_expressionText);
	}

	[Benchmark]
	public bool NcalcEval()
	{
		var expression = new Expression(_expressionText);
		expression.Functions["Al"] = (args) =>
		{
			return false;
		};

		return (bool)expression.Evaluate();
	}
}
```

DynamicExpresso always compiles the expression tree, while Ncalc uses an Expression visitor to evaluate the expression, and only compiles the expression tree if the user requires a lambda expression. Ncalc doesn't seem to support custom functions in that case, so to only consider the cost of the actual parsing in DynamicExpresso, I've added an additional benchmark that doesn't eval the expression. 

I've iterated multiple times: each commit reduces the execution time a bit. The best optim was the one that removes reflection to identify operators.

We're still a lot slower than Ncalc.

Here are the results.

### Baseline: before any optim

| Method                     | N  | Mean         | Error       | StdDev    | Median       |
|--------------------------- |--- |-------------:|------------:|----------:|-------------:|
| DynamicExpressoParsingOnly | 5  |  35,259.6 ns |   679.69 ns | 883.79 ns |  34,719.5 ns |
| DynamicExpressoEval        | 5  | 180,923.1 ns | 1,020.49 ns | 852.16 ns | 180,767.2 ns |
| NcalcEval                  | 5  |     656.7 ns |     1.81 ns |   1.60 ns |     656.8 ns |
| DynamicExpressoParsingOnly | 20 | 149,459.6 ns |   914.39 ns | 810.58 ns | 149,296.0 ns |
| DynamicExpressoEval        | 20 | 399,101.4 ns |   926.32 ns | 821.16 ns | 398,954.1 ns |
| NcalcEval                  | 20 |   2,698.5 ns |    10.08 ns |   9.42 ns |   2,701.2 ns |

### After all optimizations

| Method                     | N  | Mean         | Error       | StdDev      |
|--------------------------- |--- |-------------:|------------:|------------:|
| DynamicExpressoParsingOnly | 5  |  15,655.8 ns |   162.43 ns |   126.82 ns |
| DynamicExpressoEval        | 5  | 160,163.4 ns |   761.47 ns |   594.50 ns |
| NcalcEval                  | 5  |     640.0 ns |     9.03 ns |     9.66 ns |
| DynamicExpressoParsingOnly | 20 |  63,925.0 ns |   286.82 ns |   239.50 ns |
| DynamicExpressoEval        | 20 | 314,246.6 ns | 1,869.79 ns | 1,561.36 ns |
| NcalcEval                  | 20 |   2,798.6 ns |    12.05 ns |    10.06 ns |

For parsing only, for N=20, we went from 149us to 64us, or a 57% decrease.

